\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/pipeline.pdf}
\caption{Generational depth estimation pipeline. Seed documents are recursively passed through a generation operator $G_\delta$ to produce a depth chain. Statistical features are fed to the OBD-LightGBM classifier for depth prediction.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

A generation operator $G_\delta$ (LLM + decoding strategy $\delta$) produces successive generations $X \to Y_1 \to \cdots \to Y_K$, where $Y_k = G_\delta(Y_{k-1})$ and $Y_0 \triangleq X$ (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).
This sequence forms a Markov chain.
By the Data Processing Inequality, $I(X; Y_1) \geq I(X; Y_2) \geq \cdots \geq 0$: the original signal degrades monotonically.
Together with Markov chain convergence, this guarantees a finite $\Ktheory$ beyond which adjacent generations are indistinguishable.

We define the operational lower bound:
\begin{equation}
  \Kempirical = \max\{k \geq 1 : \text{CI}_{\text{lower}}^{95\%}(\auc_{k-1,k}) > \theta\},
  \label{eq:kstar}
\end{equation}
where $\theta = 0.60$ corresponds to Cohen's $h \approx 0.20$\footnote{$h = 2\arcsin\!\sqrt{0.60} - 2\arcsin\!\sqrt{0.50} \approx 0.2014$, a ``small'' effect size by Cohen's (1988) convention.} (a conventional small-effect-size threshold) and lies well above the permutation null distribution (95th percentile $< 0.55$ across all configurations; bootstrap: 1{,}000 resamples).
This choice is further validated by the downstream filtering experiment (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}), where $\theta{=}0.60$ captures the practically meaningful boundary between actionable and non-actionable depth distinctions.
$\Kempirical$ varies with $\theta$: at $\theta{=}0.55$, $\Kempirical{=}2$--$3$ depending on configuration ($\Kempirical{=}3$ for Qwen variants, $\Kempirical{=}2$ for Pythia and OLMo); at $\theta{=}0.65$, continuation modes drop to $\Kempirical{=}1$; at $\theta{=}0.70$, $\Kempirical{=}1$ universally (Section~\ref{sec:kstar_boundary}).

\subsection{Features and Classifier}
\label{sec:features}

Each document is characterized by a 15-dimensional feature vector spanning four groups: \emph{surprisal moments} (mean, std, skewness, kurtosis; 4~dims), \emph{surprisal derivatives} (mean and std of first- and second-order differences, plus first-order skewness; 5~dims), \emph{vocabulary diversity} (type-token ratio, hapax legomena ratio, self-BLEU; 3~dims), and \emph{frequency tail statistics} (frequency kurtosis, entropy, rare token fraction; 3~dims).
All features are computed from token-level surprisal values via an external scorer model (full definitions in Table~\ref{tab:s5_features}, Appendix).
Hand-crafted features enable cross-generator transfer without retraining and permit attribution of discriminative power to specific text properties.

We use \emph{Ordinal Binary Decomposition} (OBD), decomposing the $(K{+}1)$-class problem into $K$ cumulative binary sub-classifiers, each estimating $P(\text{depth} > k \mid \mathbf{x})$ via LightGBM (hyperparameters in Table~\ref{tab:s6_lgbm_params}).
OBD achieves lower MAE than multiclass LightGBM (e.g., 0.984 vs.\ 1.016 on Qwen-rewrite) while maintaining superior pairwise $\auc$ (mean 0.6095 vs.\ 0.4883 for ordinal logistic regression).

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate five configurations spanning three model families (Table~\ref{tab:setup}): Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (rewrite and continuation modes), Qwen2.5-7B (continuation), Pythia-1.4B and OLMo-1B (continuation).
Each uses 5{,}000 C4 seeds truncated to 256 tokens, recursively generated for $K{=}5$ iterations (30{,}000 documents per configuration).
Default decoding: nucleus $p{=}0.95$, $T{=}1.0$; decoding ablation covers greedy, $T{=}0.7$, and $T{=}1.2$.
Continuation mode truncates seeds to 128 tokens and extends to 256; rewrite mode paraphrases the full text.
Evaluation: 5-fold GroupKFold cross-validation grouped by seed document; pairwise $\auc$ as primary metric.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Experimental configurations.}
\label{tab:setup}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Config} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Mode} & $N$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-rw   & Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct & Rewrite & 5{,}000 \\
Qwen-cont & Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Pythia    & Pythia-1.4B            & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
OLMo      & OLMo-1B               & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Qwen-7B   & Qwen2.5-7B            & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Application: $\Kstar$-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

$\Kempirical = 2$ makes a falsifiable prediction: if depth assignments beyond $k{=}2$ carry no discriminative signal, graduated refinement beyond the binary boundary should provide no additional benefit---binary curation should suffice, and graduated performance should converge toward binary as the decay steepness increases.
We test this directly by comparing six data curation strategies on a corpus of 30{,}000 documents (5{,}000 per depth, depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter} (uniform weight), (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from the 15D LightGBM classifier), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson} (importance weighting following \citet{drayson2025}, same classifier, weights $w_j \propto (1 - q(x_j))^b$, $b{=}1$, cap $r_{\max}{=}3$), (iv)~\textbf{graduated} (exponential decay $w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain documents above corpus median surprisal), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir} (importance resampling against depth-0 reference).

We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs, evaluating on MMLU (14{,}042 examples, 0-shot) and HellaSwag (10{,}042 examples, 0-shot) across 5 seeds; the graduated strategy is further crossed with 5~$\alpha$ values (51 total runs).
This 100\% synthetic setting provides an upper-bound characterization; in realistic mixed-corpus settings, the filtering benefit scales with the contamination fraction~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, dohmatob2024model}.

\paragraph{Results.}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Downstream performance of six data filtering strategies. Fine-tuning: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, LoRA, 30K documents, 5 seeds. Graduated shows best $\alpha$ only; full sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.}
\label{tab:tab_filtering_main}
\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}
\end{table}

All strategies degrade absolute performance relative to the base model (MMLU 0.6018, HellaSwag 0.6821); the goal is damage minimization, not improvement.
Binary-ours minimizes MMLU degradation most effectively (Table~\ref{tab:tab_filtering_main}), reducing the gap from $-$1.53\,pp (no-filter) to $-$0.77\,pp---an approximately 50\% reduction.
Pairwise paired $t$-tests with Holm-Bonferroni correction confirm that binary-ours significantly outperforms all five alternatives on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} < 0.05$); 8 of 15 main-strategy pairs are significant on MMLU, 9 of 15 on HellaSwag (full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
The strategy ordering is interpretable through $\Kempirical$: binary filtering exploits the one reliable boundary (0v1), while graduated strategies assign different weights to depths 2--5 based on near-random classifier outputs (2v3 $\auc < \theta$), effectively injecting noise into the importance weights.
DSIR performs worst, only marginally above no-filter: its bigram feature hashing captures domain shift but not the depth-correlated distributional signatures that drive depth discrimination.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_filtering_strategies.pdf}
\caption{Downstream performance of all filtering strategies (5 seeds, $\pm$1 std). Binary-ours (green) minimizes MMLU degradation; graduated $\alpha$ sweep shows monotonic convergence toward binary. Dashed line: base model (no fine-tuning).}
\label{fig:filtering_strategies}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{$\alpha$ sensitivity.}
Higher $\alpha$ values yield monotonically better performance on both benchmarks (HellaSwag: $0.6640$ at $\alpha{=}0.1$ to $0.6701$ at $\alpha{=}0.9$).
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, graduated filtering narrows the MMLU gap to 0.49\,pp below binary-ours ($0.5892$ vs.\ $0.5941$; $p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$) but matches binary-ours exactly on HellaSwag ($0.6701$; $\Delta = 0$).
The convergence at high $\alpha$ is expected: as $\alpha \to \infty$, graduated weighting degenerates to binary thresholding.
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, depth-2 text receives only 16.5\% weight ($e^{-0.9 \times 2}$), functionally approximating a binary threshold---hence the HellaSwag equivalence, while the more noise-sensitive MMLU still detects the residual difference (detailed $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).

\paragraph{Summary.}
Binary-ours significantly outperforms all alternatives on MMLU after Holm-Bonferroni correction (8/15 main-strategy pairs significant); on HellaSwag, 9/15 pairs are significant.
On HellaSwag, graduated at $\alpha{=}0.9$ matches binary-ours exactly ($\Delta = 0$, $p_{\text{corr}} = 1.00$), consistent with steep exponential decay functionally approximating binary thresholding; graduated also significantly outperforms surprisal ($p_{\text{corr}} = .002$), while DSIR is indistinguishable from no-filter on both benchmarks.
Significance is assessed within two separate families: the 6 main strategies (15 pairs, addressing ``which strategy is best?'') and the graduated $\alpha$ sweep (``optimal $\alpha$?''), as they correspond to distinct research questions.
The benchmark-specific pattern reinforces $\Kempirical{=}2$: MMLU, which is more sensitive to importance-weight noise, confirms strict dominance of binary filtering; HellaSwag shows that even near-binary graduated weighting suffices, but finer ordinal structure offers no benefit.

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Limitations}

Our findings are bounded by (1)~model scale ($\leq$7B, with only one 7B configuration tested; the 7B condition uses Qwen2.5-7B-Base rather than Instruct, introducing a base-vs-instruct confound---though $\Kempirical{=}2$ consistency across this variation provides additional robustness evidence; cross-scale scoring rather than self-scoring), (2)~prompt-chain rather than retrain-chain protocols (our $\Kempirical$ is a conservative upper bound on retrain-chain $\Kstar$ under monotonic-degradation assumptions), (3)~English C4 text only, (4)~four decoding strategies, (5)~threshold sensitivity ($\Kempirical$ ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$ depending on configuration; all five configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at our chosen $\theta{=}0.60$), (6)~marginal boundary (Qwen-continuation 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.002; cross-scorer validation with both Pythia and OLMo scorers drops this condition to $\Kempirical{=}1$, Section~\ref{sec:transfer}), (7)~the 15D feature set yields a feature-set-specific lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher), (8)~the neural probe boundary is model-dependent ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$ for Qwen only; $2$ for Pythia/OLMo), likely reflecting self-familiarity rather than a universal signal, and (9)~while overall feature rankings are highly consistent across models ($\rho \geq 0.85$), depth-pair-level agreement weakens at the 1v2 boundary ($\tau \approx 0.37$), indicating that per-feature contributions at the critical boundary vary across models.
Detailed analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing---positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use the publicly available C4 corpus and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.

\bibliography{references}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate generational depth estimation on five model configurations:
Pythia-1.4B and OLMo-1B in continuation mode, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct in both rewrite and continuation modes, and Qwen2.5-7B in continuation mode (cross-scale scoring with Qwen2.5-1.5B).
All experiments use the OBD-LightGBM classifier with the 15-dimensional feature set (Section~\ref{sec:method}), with 5{,}000 texts per depth level (generations 0--5).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{$\Kempirical = 2$ Across All Configurations}
\label{sec:kstar_boundary}

